# 4️⃣ BAYES' THEOREM ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

This is one of the most important probability concepts for ML.

Bayes' theorem:

$$
P(A|B)=
\frac{P(B|A)P(A)}
{P(B)}
$$

It allows us to **reverse conditional probabilities**.

---

# 🧠 ELI5

Imagine:

> You know a person has a certain symptom.

You want to know:

> What's the probability they have a particular disease?

You're interested in:

$$
P(Disease|Symptom)
$$

But medical studies may give you:

$$
P(Symptom|Disease)
$$

Bayes lets you connect them.

---

# 5️⃣ BAYES EXAMPLE

Suppose:

```text
Disease prevalence = 1%
```

So:

$$
P(D)=0.01
$$

Suppose:

```text
If someone has the disease,
test is positive 90% of the time.
```

Therefore:

$$
P(Positive|Disease)=0.90
$$

Suppose healthy people have:

```text
10% false-positive rate
```

Therefore:

$$
P(Positive|Healthy)=0.10
$$

Question:

> If someone's test is positive, what's the probability they actually have the disease?

We want:

$$
P(Disease|Positive)
$$

Bayes:

$$
P(D|P)
=
\frac{P(P|D)P(D)}
{P(P)}
$$

Calculate:

$$
P(P)=
P(P|D)P(D)
+
P(P|Healthy)P(Healthy)
$$

$$
=0.9(0.01)+0.1(0.99)
$$

$$
=0.009+0.099
$$

$$
=0.108
$$

Therefore:

$$
P(D|P)=
\frac{0.009}{0.108}
\approx0.0833
$$

So:

```text
≈ 8.33%
```

### 😮 Important insight

Even with a 90% sensitive test, a positive result doesn't automatically mean a 90% chance of disease.

Why?

Because the disease is rare and false positives occur.

This is called the **base-rate effect**.

