# 7️⃣ COVARIANCE ⭐⭐⭐

Now we move from probability to relationships between variables.

Covariance answers:

> **"Do two variables tend to move together?"**

Suppose:

```text
Hours studied ↑
Exam score ↑
```

They tend to move in the same direction.

Positive covariance.

---

### Negative relationship

```text
Exercise ↑
Body fat ↓
```

They move in opposite directions.

Negative covariance.

---

### No obvious relationship

```text
Shoe size
Programming skill
```

Could have covariance close to zero.

---

# 🧠 Visual intuition

### Positive covariance

```text
Y
│          •
│       •
│    •
│ •
└──────────── X
```

Both increase.

---

### Negative covariance

```text
Y
│ •
│    •
│       •
│          •
└──────────── X
```

X increases while Y decreases.

---

# 8️⃣ COVARIANCE FORMULA

Population covariance:

$$
Cov(X,Y)=
\frac{1}{N}
\sum_{i=1}^{N}
(x_i-\mu_x)(y_i-\mu_y)
$$

Don't worry about memorizing it yet.

Understand the mechanism:

```text
X deviation from mean
        ×
Y deviation from mean
        ↓
Average
        ↓
Covariance
```

If deviations tend to have the same sign:

```text
positive
```

If opposite:

```text
negative
```

---

# 9️⃣ CORRELATION ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Covariance has a problem.

Its magnitude depends on the units.

For example:

```text
Height → cm
Salary → ₹
```

Covariance can become difficult to interpret.

Correlation solves this by normalizing covariance.

Pearson correlation:

$$
r=
\frac{Cov(X,Y)}
{\sigma_X\sigma_Y}
$$

Range:

$$
-1\leq r\leq1
$$

---

# 🧠 Interpretation

```text
r = +1
↓
Perfect positive linear relationship

r = 0
↓
No linear relationship

r = -1
↓
Perfect negative linear relationship
```

Example:

```text
Hours studied ↑
Score ↑
```

could have:

```text
r = +0.85
```

Strong positive linear relationship.

---

# 🔥 COVARIANCE vs CORRELATION

| Feature               | Covariance | Correlation |
| --------------------- | ---------- | ----------- |
| Measures relationship | ✅          | ✅           |
| Range                 | Unbounded  | -1 to +1    |
| Unit dependent        | Yes        | No          |
| Easy to interpret     | Less       | More        |
| Used in ML            | Yes        | Very common |

---

# 🚨 IMPORTANT: CORRELATION ≠ CAUSATION

This is an extremely important data-science concept.

Suppose:

```text
Ice cream sales ↑
Swimming accidents ↑
```

They are correlated.

Does ice cream cause drowning?

❌ No.

A third factor could be:

```text
Summer temperature ↑
       ↙       ↘
Ice cream    Swimming
sales ↑      ↑
```

Temperature is a **confounding variable**.

---

# 🧠 Interview answer

> Correlation indicates that two variables are statistically associated, but it does not establish that one variable causes the other. Causal conclusions generally require additional evidence, such as controlled experiments or appropriate causal analysis.

